TITLE:: NdefRackRouter
summary:: cables of the rack drawn as a flow chart
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRackGUI, Classes/NdefRackLayout, Classes/NdefRack

DESCRIPTION::
The geometry behind the rack window's strong::flow chart:: cables
(link::Classes/NdefRackGUI#*cableStyle:: code::\routed::, the default). It knows
nothing of the window or the server: it is given the modules' boxes and each
cable's two ends, and answers the corners of a line of straight runs at right
angles from one end to the other. The window makes one whenever the modules or
the cables change; you need it only to draw the rack's cables some other way.

A cable leaves its output at code::a::, on the right edge of the module it comes
from, and enters its input at code::b::, on the left edge of the other. It has one
of two shapes, and its strong::bend:: says where its middle runs are:

table::
## strong::shape:: || strong::runs:: || strong::bend::
## z || along a's row, down or up at x, along b's row into the input - only when b is right of a and nothing is in the way || code::[x]::
## u || out of the output, down or up at x1 to a lane at y, along it, then at x2 to b's row and in - a cable going backwards, or forwards past modules || code::[x1, y, x2]::
::

Without a bend the router finds one: the z whose vertical run is nearest the
input and touches no module, else the u whose lane is nearest the two rows -
between them when there is room, under the modules rather than over them about as
near. Cables from different outputs keep link::#*spacing:: pixels apart on a
shared gap or lane; when no way does, they share it - but never run through a
module. Cables from one output may share a run, so a fan out reads as a tree. A
bend given is kept, clipped so the cable still leaves its output and enters its
input going right; a z bend on a cable that now runs backwards is dropped.

code::
r = NdefRackRouter((osc: Rect(20, 20, 190, 120), lpf: Rect(270, 20, 190, 160)));
r.routeAll([(key: [\osc, \lpf, \in], from: \osc, to: \lpf, a: Point(210, 46), b: Point(270, 90))]);
// -> a Dictionary: [\osc, \lpf, \in] -> [Point(210, 46), Point(250, 46), Point(250, 90), Point(270, 90)]
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: rects
The modules' boxes, key -> link::Classes/Rect::, in the rack's coordinates.
RETURNS:: an NdefRackRouter, with no ground taken yet

METHOD:: bendOf
The bend of a cable from its corners: code::[x]:: for a z, code::[x1, y, x2]:: for a u.
ARGUMENT:: pts
The corners, as link::#-route:: answers them.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array::, or nil for any other line

METHOD:: dragBend
The bend after the run starting at index seg is dragged by delta: a vertical run
moves sideways, the lane up or down; the runs out of the output and into the
input move the vertical run beside them.
ARGUMENT:: pts
The corners.
ARGUMENT:: seg
The run, from code::pts[seg]:: to code::pts[seg + 1]::.
ARGUMENT:: delta
How far, a link::Classes/Point::.
RETURNS:: the new bend

METHOD:: movable
The runs a drag moves, as indexes into the corners: the middle ones.
ARGUMENT:: pts
The corners.

METHOD:: nearestRun
The run nearest a point, and how far it is.
ARGUMENT:: pts
The corners.
ARGUMENT:: point
A link::Classes/Point::.
RETURNS:: code::[index, distance]::

METHOD:: segmentDistance
How far a point is from the line from q1 to q2.
ARGUMENT:: p
The point.
ARGUMENT:: q1
One end.
ARGUMENT:: q2
The other.

METHOD:: simplify
The corners without repeats, and without corners on a straight run - what is
drawn.
ARGUMENT:: pts
The corners.

METHOD:: stub
How far a cable runs out of its output, and into its input, before it turns. 16.

METHOD:: spacing
How far apart runs of cables from different outputs keep. 8.

METHOD:: clearance
How far a cable keeps from a module. 4.

METHOD:: laneClearance
How far a lane keeps from a module. 14.

METHOD:: over
What a lane over the rows costs against one under them, in pixels: a lane over
them is taken only when it is that much nearer. 1000: a return runs under its row.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: routeAll
Every cable's corners. Cables with a bend are laid first, so the others keep
clear of them.
ARGUMENT:: cables
Events: code::(key:, from:, to:, a:, b:, bend:):: - bend optional; code::from::
and code::to:: are keys of the boxes.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Dictionary::, key -> corners

METHOD:: route
One cable's corners, from a to b; it then takes up its ground, and cables routed
after it keep clear of it.
ARGUMENT:: a
Where it leaves its output.
ARGUMENT:: b
Where it enters its input.
ARGUMENT:: from
The key of the module it comes from.
ARGUMENT:: to
The key of the module it goes into.
ARGUMENT:: bend
A bend to keep, or nil.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/Point::s

METHOD:: rects
The boxes it was given.
